@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+---
+title: How PostHog merged 709 self-driving PRs into PostHog in one month
+customer: PostHog
+featuredImage: >-
+  https://res.cloudinary.com/dmukukwp6/image/upload/TODO_posthog_1920x1080.png
+date: 2026-11-16
+seo:
+  metaTitle: How PostHog runs self-driving on its own codebase
+  metaDescription: In October, PostHog merged 709 PRs that started in its own self-driving inbox. Here's the loop, the numbers, and where it still needs a human.
+---
+
+[PostHog](https://posthog.com) is the context layer for your product, installed by more than 500,000 organizations, and the company builds PostHog on it.
+
+The PostHog app, the [PostHog MCP](/mcp), [PostHog Desktop](/desktop), and this website all report into PostHog's own project. More than 90 [scouts](/docs/self-driving/scouts) watch that project, most of them every hour.
+
+Andy Maguire used PostHog as a customer before he worked on it. In 2022, [Netdata's case study](/customers/netdata) quoted him as its analytics and machine learning lead. Today he's on PostHog's self-driving team, building the loops that turn product data into pull requests, and he runs them on PostHog first.
+
+Those loops exist because PostHog ships more code than its engineers can watch. In January 2026, the team merged 1,441 PRs. By August it was 4,869, with engineering headcount up only 10%. The extra PRs brought more errors, more agent feedback, and more Slack threads asking "is this happening to anyone else?"
+
+<OSQuote customer="posthog" author="andy_maguire" quote={0} />
+
+## Agents open most of the PRs now
+
+Over four months this year, the share of PostHog's monorepo PRs opened by agents went from around 20% to 70%. Most of those still start with an engineer prompting an agent. The newest slice is [self-driving](/self-driving): PRs that start because a scout or signal source found the problem first.
+
+The loop is the one customers get. Scouts and [signal sources](/docs/self-driving/inbox/sources) like [Error Tracking](/error-tracking), [Logs](/logs), and [Session Replay](/session-replay) emit signals. Signals group into [reports](/docs/self-driving/reports) in the [Inbox](/docs/self-driving/inbox). An agent investigates each report and, when it's actionable, opens a PR with the evidence attached.
+
+In October, the PostHog GitHub app opened 1,612 PRs from inbox reports on the [main PostHog repo](https://github.com/PostHog/posthog), and the team merged 709 of them, about one in ten of all PRs merged that month. In June, the app's PRs were 2.6% of merges.
+
+![Merged PRs opened by the PostHog GitHub app, as a share of all merged PRs in PostHog/posthog](/images/customers/posthog/posthog-agent-pr-share.png)
+
+<Caption>Merged PRs opened by the PostHog GitHub app in PostHog/posthog, as a share of all merged PRs, June to October 2026. Counted from public GitHub data and drawn as a trends insight.</Caption>
+
+Each of those PRs records the scout or source that found the problem, a link to the inbox report, whether a person or an auto-start kicked it off, and which agent, model, and skills wrote it. The repo is public, so anyone can read them.
+
+## From an agent's complaint to production in under eight hours
+
+On August 28 at 11:28 UTC, an agent working in a customer's project got stuck. It wanted to define events before any had been captured, but the MCP could only update event definitions, not create them. So it filed `missing_tool` feedback through the MCP and fell back to an ugly workaround: firing one seed event per definition.
+
+A scout called `signals-scout-agent-feedback` reads that feedback stream. About five minutes later, after checking for existing reports, open PRs, and assigned issues, it filed a report. At 11:42, an agent in PostHog Desktop opened [PR #90832](https://github.com/PostHog/posthog/pull/90832) to add an `event-definition-create` tool.
+
+![The agent feedback report in the PostHog inbox beside the pull request it became](/images/customers/posthog/posthog-report-to-pr.png)
+
+<Caption>The report holds the evidence and the suggested fix. The PR links back to it and names the agent, model, and skills that wrote it.</Caption>
+
+The first version needed work. Codex and ReviewHog, PostHog's internal review bot, sent it through rounds of review, and it picked up backend changes and tests along the way. Andy approved it at 14:22, it merged at 18:02, and it was in production by 18:56.
+
+After the merge, the scout kept watching. It notes in its scratchpad what it reported and what shipped, and it closes the theme only once the complaints have stopped.
+
+## More than 90 scouts, one inbox
+
+Some of the scouts on PostHog's project come from the built-in set every customer gets. Dozens were written by the teams who own the problem:
+
+- `signals-scout-team-self-driving` reads the team's Slack channel for bug reports that would otherwise die in a thread.
+- `signals-scout-self-driving-github-issues` sweeps the team's GitHub issues and starts work on the ones with a clear scope and nothing blocking them.
+- `signals-scout-self-driving-logs` reads production logs and adds new evidence to an existing report instead of opening a duplicate.
+- A [Replay Vision](/replay-vision) scanner watches recordings of PostHog Desktop for people getting visibly stuck.
+
+![The scouts roster for PostHog's own project](/images/customers/posthog/posthog-scouts-roster.png)
+
+<Caption>A slice of the scouts running on PostHog's own project. Each one is a skill with a schedule, so its logic is plain English anyone on the team can edit.</Caption>
+
+Because every scout is a [skill](/docs/skills), changing what one looks for doesn't need a deploy. Skills created in PostHog's internal project went from 62 in late April to 761 by the end of September, and Andy's agents loaded one in 78% of his chats across a recent 28-day stretch.
+
+<OSQuote customer="posthog" author="andy_maguire" quote={1} />
+
+Natalia Amorim on the marketing team writes scouts too. She runs two over PostHog's 90-odd comparison pages: one watches competitors' pricing and docs, and the other checks PostHog's own pages against the changelog, docs, and open PRs, because one embarrassing way to get a comparison wrong is underselling yourself. Findings arrive in Slack and the inbox with a suggested edit, and a person decides whether it becomes a PR. Across the whole website repo, the PostHog GitHub app had 68 PRs merged in October.
+
+<OSQuote customer="posthog" author="natalia_amorim" quote={0} />
+
+## Fewer than half the auto-started PRs get merged
+
+Of October's self-driving PRs that have since been merged or closed, the ones that started automatically after a report was triaged were merged about 41% of the time. When a person picked the report from the inbox first, that rose to about 82%. The rest were closed without merging.
+
+Some problems need a human from the start. On September 5, an MCP error-rate alert fired at 9.9%, against a usual 0.3% to 1.2%. The investigation traced it to a mismatch between two separately deployed workers, and Andy steered the fix himself.
+
+<OSQuote customer="posthog" author="andy_maguire" quote={2} />
+
+The team also checks whether the extra PRs are breaking production. Reverts were 0.2% of merged PRs in June, in line with the 0.1% to 0.4% of the year before, when people wrote most of the code. GitHub data streams into the [context warehouse](/warehouse) too, and the team asks [PostHog AI](/ai) which CI jobs cost the most and fail most often.
+
+For now, Andy still reviews the reports and the PRs himself.
+
+<OSQuote customer="posthog" author="andy_maguire" quote={3} />
